23:27:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: A jacket can be both waterproof and breathable thanks to specially engineered fabrics that block liquid water (rain) while allowing water vapour (sweat) to escape. The secret lies in the size of the pores or the chemical structure of the membrane used. There are two main technologies: ### 1. Microporous membranes (e.g., Gore-Tex, eVent) These have tiny pores – around 0.2 to 10 microns in diameter. - A water droplet from rain is huge in comparison (about 100–5000 microns), so it cannot pass through. - Water vapour molecules are incredibly small (about 0.0004 microns), so they can easily slip through the pores and escape to the outside. The result: you stay dry from rain, but sweat can evaporate before you feel clammy. ### 2. Hydrophilic (non-porous) membranes (e.g., Sympatex, some PU coatings) These have no holes. Instead, they use a solid, water-loving (hydrophilic) polymer. - On the inside, the membrane absorbs moisture vapour from your body and transports it molecule by molecule to the outside, driven by the difference in humidity and temperature (it’s a chemical diffusion process). - Because there are no physical pores, liquid water can’t get through, and the membrane remains completely waterproof. Many modern jackets combine both approaches or add a durable water repellent (DWR) coating on the outer fabric to bead water off, so the membrane can breathe more efficiently. In short, physics or chemistry makes it possible to separate liquid water from water vapour, keeping rain out while letting sweat escape.
